#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Read the published missal, catena and corpus in a terminal.

A reader for the same structure the browser pages read, laid out as a
dashboard: eight surfaces, one per page of the site, each reached by its digit.

This tool derives nothing. Every view is read from `src/web/data/` or asked
of the browser's own models and renderers, run under node in one process set
up as a page's realm (`scripts/lectern/bridge.js`), so what a reader sees here
and what the site renders cannot disagree about anything but layout.

TWO VERBS.

`open` is the interactive reader. It needs a terminal and the Textual library
(`requirements-tui.txt`, or `pacman -S python-textual`). Reading a web edition
in full also needs the Markdown the site pins (`requirements-public-alpha.txt`);
without it the texts surface says so where the text would be.

`snapshot` renders the same reader headlessly at a fixed size, once every
pending load has settled, and prints the screen: as plain text, as text with
ANSI colour, or as SVG. It is how the reader is checked without a terminal, and
the transcripts below are its output.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

